# Kit SDK Update Procedure

How to update the Kit SDK to a new version (e.g. 110.0 → 110.1).

---

## Upstream Reference

The canonical upstream is **[NVIDIA-Omniverse/kit-app-template](https://github.com/NVIDIA-Omniverse/kit-app-template)** on the `main` branch. Check its `CHANGELOG.md` and `tools/` files for each release.

Official release notes: `https://docs.omniverse.nvidia.com/dev-guide/latest/release-notes/<version>.html`

---

## Files to Update

### 1. Kit Kernel Version — `kit-app-template-main/tools/deps/kit-sdk.packman.xml`

The `kit-kernel` package version string. Only the semver prefix changes; the `+feature.${platform_target_abi}.${config}` suffix is a packman variable.

```xml
<package name="kit-kernel" version="110.1.1+production.${platform_target_abi}.${config}"/>
```

Note: between 110.1.0 and 110.1.1 the suffix changed from `+feature` to `+production` — the upstream now ships production-tagged packages.

### 2. VERSION.md — `kit-app-template-main/tools/VERSION.md`

Single line with the semver (e.g. `110.1.1`). Used for packaging metadata.

### 3. Repo Tool Dependencies — `kit-app-template-main/tools/deps/repo-deps.packman.xml`

Bump each package version to match the upstream template. Compare with:
```
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/NVIDIA-Omniverse/kit-app-template/main/tools/deps/repo-deps.packman.xml
```

Current versions (110.1.1) — unchanged from 110.1.0:

| Package | Version |
|---------|---------|
| repo_man | 2.2.1 |
| repo_build | 1.27.2 |
| repo_kit_tools | 1.18.1 |
| repo_test | 2.46.1 |
| repo_package | 6.2.0 |
| repo_kit_template | 2.3.2 |
| repo_usd | 5.0.29 |

### 4. repo.toml — `kit-app-template-main/repo.toml`

Diff the upstream `repo.toml` against ours. Merge new sections/settings while preserving our custom `[repo_precache_exts].apps` list. Common additions in updates: new deprecation warnings, new tool sections, new settings.

**Do NOT adopt `generated_app_path`** from the upstream template — it auto-generates a kit file from all local extensions and fails when test dependencies reference `.kit` app names (e.g. `younite.usd_viewer_streaming` in the setup extension's `[[test]]` block). Our project uses explicit `apps = [...]` entries instead.

### 5. kit-sdk-deps.packman.xml — `kit-app-template-main/tools/deps/kit-sdk-deps.packman.xml`

Usually unchanged between minor versions. Compare with upstream to verify.

---

## Generated Blocks in .kit Files

Each `.kit` file in `source/apps/` contains an auto-generated `BEGIN GENERATED PART` / `END GENERATED PART` block that locks extension versions. These blocks are pinned to the old SDK version after an update.

**Action:** Delete the entire generated block (from `BEGIN` to `END` inclusive) from each `.kit` file. The build tooling (`repo precache_exts`) regenerates them with correct versions on the next build.

**Important:** The base `.kit` file must have a `[settings.app.exts] folders.'++' = [...]` block **outside** the generated section. The old 110.0 generated block included these search paths, but the 110.1 regenerated block only contains `enabled = [...]`. Without explicit folders, production launches (`startstream_prod`) fail because Kit can't find local extensions. The dev `.kit` has its own folders block that masks this issue when using `startstream.bat`.

Files with generated blocks:
- `younite.usd_viewer_streaming_base.kit`
- `younite.usd_viewer_streaming_dev.kit`
- `younite.usd_composer.kit`

---

## Clean Build

After all file changes, run `cleanbuild.bat` from the project root (or manually delete `_build/` and `_repo/` inside `kit-app-template-main/`). Then run `startstream.bat` to perform a fresh build.

The first build after an SDK update is slower because packman downloads the new Kit kernel and all updated extensions.

---

## After-Pull Workflow (for other developers)

When another developer pulls a Kit SDK update from git:

1. Run `cleanbuild.bat` from the project root
2. Run `startstream.bat` as normal

The build will fetch the new SDK version, download updated extensions, and regenerate the `.kit` file version locks automatically.

> **⚠️ Common gotcha after a SDK bump + clean build:** if a teammate runs `startstream_prod.bat` *first* (skipping dev), they may see red "fallback" materials with `[omni.rtx.materials] Unable to find SdrShaderNode` errors. This happens when the prod resolution closure doesn't reach an extension that prod actually needs at runtime — a clean `_build/` plus a SDK-bumped registry can change which extensions get pulled transitively. **Fix:** add the missing extension(s) to `younite.usd_viewer_streaming_base.kit` `[dependencies]` so prod doesn't depend on transitive resolution. We've already done this for the MDL chain (`omni.mdl`, `omni.mdl.neuraylib`, `omni.mdl.usd_converter`, `omni.kit.material.library`, `omni.kit.usd.mdl`). See `streaming-profiles.mdc` § *Critical pitfall: production kit must explicitly list every runtime-required extension* for the full triage procedure.

---

## Extension Checklist

After updating, verify that all local extensions still have the 5 required files (see `streaming-profiles.mdc`). Missing `premake5.lua` is a common issue — without it, the build won't symlink the extension into `_build/` and Kit can't discover it at runtime.

Also check for empty extension stub folders in `source/extensions/` that may have been left behind from removed features. These cause harmless but noisy warnings during build.

---

## Driver Requirements

Kit SDK updates may bump the CUDA toolkit version, which raises the minimum GPU driver requirement. Check the release notes for minimum driver versions before deploying.

Kit 110.1 requires:
- **Windows:** driver >= 572.61
- **Linux:** driver >= 570.124.06

